  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1012 PM CST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nTonight and Wednesday...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this evening reveals mid-\nlevel ridging over the Desert Southwest. Closer to home, a \nshortwave trough is pushing across the northern and into the\ncentral Plains before getting swept into an amplifying trough \nover the eastern 2/3rds of the CONUS. At the surface, an \nassociated low is currently situated in southeast South Dakota \nbefore tracking east- southeast into central Iowa by midday \nWednesday. This feature will sweep an associated cold front \nthrough the area overnight, shifting low- level flow from \nsoutherly to northwesterly. Any meaningful moisture and \nprecipitation chances will remain to our north and east. An \nincreasing pressure gradient on the back side of the low will \nbring northwesterly wind gusts of 25-35 mph to the area through \nmuch of the day Wednesday. As cooler air filters into the area, \nhigh temperatures will drop 5-10 degrees from the previous day, \npeaking in the mid 50s to low 60s.\n\nThursday through Saturday...\n\nContinued northwesterly flow aloft will bring cooler temperatures to \nthe region to end the work week. High temperatures are expected to \npeak in the 40s daily Thursday through at least Saturday. Mostly \nclear skies will allow for efficient radiational cooling overnight, \nallowing Thursday morning lows to drop into the 30s, with \ntemperatures dipping just below freezing across northeast \nNebraska. Friday morning continues to trend on the cooler side, \nwith lows dipping into the low to mid 20s across the area. Omaha\nwill likely see it's first freezing temperatures of the season \nFriday morning, which would tie our record for the latest first \nfreeze set back in 2015. Saturday morning will see freezing \ntemperatures yet again, with lows dipping back into the 20s. \nWhile the end of the week will be on the cooler side, calm and \ndry conditions are expected are expected to prevail thanks to a \nbroad corridor of surface high pressure in place.\n\nSunday and Beyond...\n\nLate Saturday into Sunday, a fleeting corridor of upper-level \nridging will be interrupted by a mid-level low expected to eject\nfrom the Desert Southwest into the southern and central Plains.\nLong- range deterministic guidance continues to track this \nfeature across KS/OK, with a broad precipitation shield grazing \nfar southeast NE/southwest IA. GEFS and EC/EC-AIFS members show \nsome variation in this track, with about 1/3rd of the members \nbringing the feature further north with increased precipitation \nchances locally. Nearly all EC/EC-AIFS members keep the \nprecipitation as rain, however, GEFS brings cooler air on the \nback side of the system, with approx. 1/4th of the members \nbrining some light snow. \n\nWith that being said, PoPs currently peak at 15-30% Sunday morning \ninto the afternoon. The latest forecast package continues to bring \nonly rain, though morning temperatures will be just a few degrees \nabove freezing as precipitation moves into southeast NE. We will \ncontinue to monitor trends as the system approaches. Overall, \nmoisture appears fairly limited, with QPF topping out at a few \nhundredths of an inch across southeast NE. High temperatures on \nSunday are expected to peak in the mid to upper 40s. High \ntemperatures will warm into the low 50's to start the next work \nweek, though long range guidance has trended a bit cooler on next \nweeks \"warm up\". Looking ahead, a more amplified flow pattern with \nadditional precipitation chances is expected to arrive by mid-\nweek.",
